#!/bin/sh
# pre-commit.hook v3 — 2026-08-17. v1 AGENT-1 (H15); v2 ATTACKER-1 (H35);
# v3 ok-1 (H94).
#
# ==== v3, H94 — the defect removed ==========================================
# CLASS: A COMPLETED RECORD CAN LEAVE AN APPEND-MOSTLY DOCUMENT AND EVERY GATE
# IN THIS REPO STAYS GREEN. Commit `10ed3f2` deleted 177 lines and four whole
# `## Cycle` entries from `HANDOFF.ok-1.md` -- its author's own evidence for
# four cycles -- and passed all three checks below, plus `commit-msg`, CLEAN.
# None of them looks at what a commit REMOVES.
#
# Added: `spikes/harness/recordloss.py`. It compares HEAD's blob against the
# INDEX blob for the paths in the commit, so unlike the two document checkers
# above it is not a function of the shared tree and cannot be tripped by another
# lane's uncommitted edit (H72). Replaying every committed revision of the five
# journals and CHANNEL.md finds 2 refusals, both read and both real -- so it is
# added with no backlog, which is the H14 condition this header sets for adding
# anything here. The revision count is deliberately not quoted: it grows with
# the history, and `python3 spikes/harness/recordloss.py --history` prints it
# next to the HEAD it was taken at.
# Cites: file:MISSION_LOOP.md "12.3"
# ===========================================================================
#
# ==== v2, AND IT IS A CORRECTION AGAINST v1's HEADER (§12.7) ================
# DEFECT REMOVED: THE GATE JUDGED THE WORKING TREE WHILE SAYING IT JUDGED THE
# COMMIT. v1's header below ends "The gate judges the content of your commit,
# and on a shared file that content is not only yours." `refcheck.py` and
# `journalcheck.py` read files with plain `open()`, so their verdict is a
# function of the TREE. Both halves measured in `spikes/H35_gate_scope/`, run
# against v1, four controls held:
#
#   F1  the gate PASSED a commit whose own blob carried a duplicate WORK_QUEUE
#       row id -- `git add` the broken file, repair the tree copy, commit the
#       index. refcheck check 5 exists to refuse exactly that content and was
#       inert on it. Family C, the artifact is not what you think, sitting in
#       the gate. FIXED HERE, fail-closed: see UNSOUND below.
#   F2  the gate REFUSED a `git commit --only <unrelated path>` whose content
#       does not include the broken file at all. v1's header records this exact
#       scenario -- "one lane blocked by another lane's UNCOMMITTED edit" -- as
#       investigated and disproved. The disproof holds only for a SHARED file,
#       where `--only` does commit the co-editor's edits to that file. The
#       general case needs no shared file and it reproduces. NOT "FIXED": see
#       the F2 paragraph below. The reading is what died, not the design.
#
# THE UNSOUNDNESS GUARD, and why it is a refusal and not a materialization.
# Making the gate judge the commit exactly means running the checkers on the
# committed content. Materializing it is `git checkout-index -a --prefix=`, and
# that was MEASURED rather than assumed: 614 ms, 164 MB, 3482 files per commit,
# on top of three lanes. So v2 does not materialize. It computes the exact
# condition under which the cheap tree-based verdict IS the commit's verdict --
# every path in the commit has the same content in the tree as in the index --
# and REFUSES when that fails, naming the paths. A gate that cannot see the
# artifact must refuse, not guess: that is family B's remedy applied to itself.
# The guard cannot fire under `git commit --only`, which §13 mandates, because
# `--only` takes those paths FROM the tree; it fires on `git add` + edit +
# `git commit`, which is the workflow §13 forbids and nothing enforced until now.
#
# WHY F2 IS DOCUMENTED AND NOT REMOVED. Scoping the two document checkers to the
# commit's paths would make a duplicate row id in a shared WORK_QUEUE.md
# invisible to every lane not committing that file, and v1's own experience is
# that the fleet-wide reading caught a real duplicate `H27`. Narrowing a gate is
# not a defect I get to fix by weakening it (§10). So the behaviour stands and
# the DESCRIPTION is corrected: for `refcheck.py` and `journalcheck.py` this is a
# gate on the state of the shared documents in the tree, which any lane can trip
# and any lane can clear. `githygiene.py` is different -- it reads the INDEX via
# `git ls-files` and is already scoped to the commit. What v2 adds is
# DIAGNOSABILITY: a refusal now prints the paths your commit actually carries, so
# a blocked lane can see in one line whether the violation is even its own.
#
# VERIFIED MECHANICALLY rather than by eye (§12.4): git exports `GIT_INDEX_FILE`
# to `pre-commit` -- `.git/index` for a plain commit, `.git/next-index-<pid>.lock`
# for `git commit --only` -- so `git diff --cached` inside this hook reads the
# index that is about to become the commit, under both workflows.
# Cites: man:githooks "pre-commit"
# Cites: file:MISSION_LOOP.md "13"
# ===========================================================================
#
# v1 header follows, unedited except where marked.
#
# EVERY MECHANICAL CHECK IN THIS REPO WAS ADVISORY. H15's row: "a check that
# REPORTS but does not GATE is a stronger version of a prose NOTE, not a
# different kind of thing." The trailer gate (`commit-msg.hook`) was the single
# exception, and it checks trailers only. `refcheck.py`, `journalcheck.py` and
# `githygiene.py` ran when somebody remembered, which on the evidence is: the
# cycle that wrote them, and not the next one. I added two of the three in the
# hour before writing this, so this is my debt before it is anyone's row.
#
# WHY pre-commit AND NOT commit-msg
# §13.1 records that this gate was installed as `pre-commit` once and refused
# EVERY commit -- because the TRAILER check needs the message, and `man
# githooks` gives `pre-commit` no parameters. That is an argument about the
# trailer check specifically. These three checks read the tree and the index and
# need no message at all, so `pre-commit` is where they belong, and putting them
# in `commit-msg` would run them after the author has already been asked for a
# message. Both hooks now exist and neither duplicates the other.
#
# WHY IT REFUSES RATHER THAN REPORTS, AND WHY THAT IS SAFE TODAY
# H14's failure mode is a checker that fires on a known-accepted backlog every
# run, so everyone learns to bypass it -- `githygiene.py` was exit 1 permanently
# on 16 already-tracked binaries and its verdict therefore carried no
# information. That is NOT the situation here, and it was checked rather than
# assumed: at install time all three are green on this tree
# (`refcheck` 31 files resolve, `journalcheck` 2 journals clean, `githygiene`
# "clean -- nothing you are about to commit violates §13"). With no backlog, a
# refusal can only mean the commit in front of it introduced something.
#
# So there is deliberately NO baseline-diffing machinery here. If a backlog ever
# accumulates, the upgrade is to compare against HEAD and refuse only on new
# items -- but building that today would be scaffolding for a backlog that does
# not exist, and it would have to be maintained by every lane meanwhile.
# ponytail: no baseline diff, add one if these ever go red on a backlog.
#
# THIS GATE REFUSED ITS OWN AUTHOR'S NEXT COMMIT, TEN MINUTES AFTER INSTALL, and
# the first reading was wrong in a way worth keeping. Two lanes had numbered a
# WORK_QUEUE row `H27` concurrently and refcheck refused the duplicate. That
# looked like the fleet-stop this header argues against -- one lane blocked by
# another lane's UNCOMMITTED edit -- and a HEAD baseline was measured (1.44 s and
# 161 MB of worktree per commit) before the reading was checked. It was wrong:
# `git commit --only` on a SHARED file commits the other lane's in-flight edits
# to that file as well, which is §13's blast-radius point, so the duplicate was
# genuinely in the content being authored and refusing was correct.
# >>> CORRECTED 2026-08-17 (ATTACKER-1, H35) under §12.4 and §5. The next
# >>> sentence read "The gate judges the content of your commit, and on a shared
# >>> file that content is not only yours." The first clause is false and the
# >>> H35 probe refutes it in both directions (F1, F2 above). The gate judges the
# >>> TREE for two of its three checkers. The paragraph above is otherwise
# >>> correct and is why F2 is documented rather than removed.
# Measured before believed; the machinery was not built.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED ON THE CHECKS, FAIL-OPEN ON THE ENVIRONMENT. A checker that is
# missing or that cannot start is NOT a violation -- it is a broken clone, and
# refusing there would wedge every lane over a `python3` that moved. A checker
# that RUNS and returns non-zero refuses. The distinction is the whole reason
# this file has a selfcheck.
#
# Bypass is `git commit --no-verify`, deliberately and on the record: the hook
# is shared by every lane in this working tree, so an emergency must not need a
# lane to edit a gate the other lanes are also standing behind.
#
# Installed by spikes/harness/install_hooks.sh; drift-checked by
# spikes/harness/test_loop_gate.sh. Selfcheck: sh pre-commit.hook --selfcheck
# Finding and falsifiers: spikes/H35_gate_scope/ (sh probe.sh [alt-hook]).
set -e
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

CHECKS='spikes/harness/refcheck.py
spikes/harness/journalcheck.py
spikes/harness/githygiene.py
spikes/harness/recordloss.py'

# Paths this commit carries whose WORKING-TREE copy is not the copy being
# committed. While that set is empty the tree-based verdict below is exactly the
# commit's verdict; while it is not, the checkers are reading a different
# artifact and the gate must say so rather than guess.
unsound_paths() {
  _in=$(git diff --cached --name-only 2>/dev/null || true)
  [ -n "$_in" ] || return 0
  git diff --name-only 2>/dev/null | while IFS= read -r p; do
    if [ -n "$p" ] && printf '%s\n' "$_in" | grep -qxF -- "$p"; then
      printf '%s\n' "$p"
    fi
  done
}

run_checks() {
  _u=$(unsound_paths || true)
  if [ -n "$_u" ]; then
    echo "pre-commit REFUSED — the content you are committing is not the content on disk:"
    printf '  %s\n' $_u
    echo "  These checkers read the tree, so their verdict would be about a file"
    echo "  you are not committing. Stage what you mean:  git commit --only <paths>"
    echo "  (MISSION_LOOP §13). Bypass: git commit --no-verify"
    return 1
  fi
  _bad=''
  for c in $CHECKS; do
    [ -f "$c" ] || { echo "  SKIP  $c is absent — broken clone, not a violation"; continue; }
    if out=$(python3 "$c" 2>&1); then :; else
      _bad="$_bad $c"
      echo "----- $c REFUSED -----"
      echo "$out" | tail -12
    fi
  done
  if [ -n "$_bad" ]; then
    echo "pre-commit REFUSED —$_bad"
    # F2 diagnosability: refcheck and journalcheck judge the SHARED TREE, so the
    # violation may be in a file this commit does not carry. Print what it does
    # carry, so a blocked lane does not have to guess whose edit stopped it.
    echo "  your commit carries: $(git diff --cached --name-only 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ')"
    echo "  refcheck/journalcheck judge the shared tree, not only these paths."
    echo "Bypass: git commit --no-verify"
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

if [ "$1" = "--selfcheck" ]; then
  # The property that matters is REACHABILITY OF A RED RUN (H7's question: a
  # passing suite cannot tell you a failure is expressible). Driven with stubs,
  # so it asserts the hook's own logic and not the current state of the tree.
  fail=0
  CHECKS=/nonexistent/definitely_absent.py
  run_checks >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail=1
  [ "$fail" = 0 ] || { echo "  FAIL  an absent checker must SKIP, not refuse"; }
  fail2=0
  d=$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks)/_pc_selfcheck.$$
  mkdir -p "$d"
  printf 'import sys\nsys.exit(3)\n' > "$d/red.py"
  printf 'import sys\nsys.exit(0)\n' > "$d/green.py"
  CHECKS="$d/green.py"
  run_checks >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail2=1
  [ "$fail2" = 0 ] || echo "  FAIL  a green checker must pass"
  fail3=0
  CHECKS="$d/red.py"
  run_checks >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail3=1
  [ "$fail3" = 1 ] || echo "  FAIL  a RED checker must refuse — the gate is inert"

  # v2, H35. The guard above is worthless if it cannot fire, and a guard whose
  # falsifier is "the current tree happens to be clean" is family A. So drive it
  # in a THROWAWAY repo with a real staged-then-edited file, and drive the
  # negative case too: a `--only` commit with an unrelated dirty tracked file
  # must NOT trip it, or the guard would block the workflow §13 mandates.
  fail4=0; fail5=0; fail6=0
  t="$d/repo"; mkdir -p "$t"
  (
    cd "$t"
    git init -q .; git config user.email a@b; git config user.name a
    echo one > f.md; echo two > g.md
    git add f.md g.md; git commit -q -m base
    echo staged > f.md; git add f.md; echo 'EDITED AFTER STAGING' > f.md
    export GIT_INDEX_FILE="$t/.git/index"
    exit 0
  )
  # `unsound_paths` and `run_checks` are functions in THIS shell, so run them
  # with cwd inside the throwaway repo rather than reimplementing them there.
  CHECKS=''
  ( cd "$t" && u=$(unsound_paths); [ "$u" = "f.md" ] ) || fail4=1
  [ "$fail4" = 0 ] || echo "  FAIL  staged-then-edited f.md must be reported unsound (got: $(cd "$t" && unsound_paths | tr '\n' ' '))"
  ( cd "$t" && run_checks >/dev/null 2>&1 ) && fail5=1
  [ "$fail5" = 0 ] || echo "  FAIL  an unsound path must REFUSE the commit"
  ( cd "$t" && git checkout -q -- f.md 2>/dev/null; git reset -q; echo dirty >> g.md
    u=$(unsound_paths); [ -z "$u" ] ) || fail6=1
  [ "$fail6" = 0 ] || echo "  FAIL  a dirty file that is NOT in the commit must not be reported unsound"

  rm -rf "$d"
  bad=$(( fail + fail2 + (1 - fail3) + fail4 + fail5 + fail6 ))
  [ "$bad" = 0 ] && echo "pre-commit selfcheck: red reachable, green passes, absent skips, unsound refuses, unrelated dirt does not"
  [ "$bad" = 0 ]
  exit $?
fi

run_checks
